{
  "title": "CSCI-142 Foundations: Object-Oriented Design & Data Structures",
  "learningObjectives": [
    "Identify and apply core object-oriented design principles (cohesion, coupling, inheritance) to structure code for modifiability and extensibility.",
    "Select appropriate abstract data types (stacks, queues, trees, graphs) and implement them using object-oriented collection libraries.",
    "Design and implement exception handling and input/output streams within a modern IDE to manage runtime errors and user interaction."
  ],
  "beats": [
    {
      "title": "1. Problem Decomposition and Algorithmic Foundations",
      "claim": "Programming begins with precise problem statements, input and output examples, and algorithms written independently of syntax, where functions decompose solutions into named, self-","example": "To sort a list of student grades, first define the input (unsorted list) and output (sorted list), then design a recursive merge algorithm before writing any Python code.",
      "visualDirection": "Display a flowchart showing the decomposition of a complex problem into smaller sub-problems, with arrows pointing from the main problem to three distinct function blocks labeled 'Input Validation', 'Core Algorithm', and 'Output Formatting'","sourceIds": ["opendsa-programming-foundations"]
    },
    {
      "title": "2. Data Structures: Trees and Graphs",
      "claim": "Trees represent hierarchy and graphs represent connected entities; both are fundamental structures for organizing data in object-oriented systems, supporting nested data structures","example": "A family tree is modeled as a tree where each node is a Person object, while a social network is modeled as a graph where nodes are User objects and edges represent friendships.","visualDirection": "Render a side-by-side comparison: left side shows a binary tree diagram with labeled nodes, right side shows a directed graph with nodes and weighted edges","sourceIds": ["opendsa-programming-foundations", "rit-csci-142"]
    },
    {
      "title": "3. Objects, Classes, and Inheritance",
      "claim": "Python objects have identity, a type, and a value; classes define these attributes and behaviors, and inheritance allows subclasses to reuse and extend parent functionality","example": "Define a base class 'Vehicle' with attributes 'speed' and 'color'; create a subclass 'ElectricCar' that inherits these and adds a 'battery_level' attribute.","visualDirection": "Show a UML class diagram with a 'Vehicle' class containing attributes and methods, and an arrow pointing from 'ElectricCar' to 'Vehicle' labeled 'extends'","sourceIds": ["python-language-reference", "rit-csci-142"]
    },
    {
      "title": "4. Interfaces and Abstract Data Types",
      "claim": "Interfaces define contracts for behavior without implementation details, enabling polymorphism; object-oriented collection libraries provide ready-made implementations for stacks,","example": "Define an interface 'QueueInterface' with methods 'enqueue' and 'dequeue'; use the built-in collections.deque as the concrete implementation for a queue in a banking system.","visualDirection": "Illustrate a contract diagram where multiple classes (e.g., 'BankAccount', 'Transaction') implement the 'QueueInterface' box, with dotted lines indicating the interface relationship","sourceIds": ["rit-csci-142"]
    },
    {
      "title": "5. Static vs. Dynamic Data Types",
      "claim": "Static types are determined at compile time and enforce strict structure, while dynamic types (like Python) are resolved at runtime, allowing flexible object manipulation","example": "In a statically typed language, a variable declared as 'int' cannot hold a string; in Python, the same variable can be reassigned to any object type without recompilation.","visualDirection": "Split screen: left side shows a compiler error message for a type mismatch, right side shows a Python REPL where a variable changes type seamlessly","sourceIds": ["python-language-reference", "rit-csci-142"]
    },
    {
      "title": "6. Software Qualities: Cohesion and Coupling",
      "claim": "High cohesion means a class has a single, well-defined responsibility; low coupling means classes depend minimally on each other, both critical for modifiability","example": "A 'PaymentProcessor' class should only handle payment logic (high cohesion) and should not directly instantiate 'EmailSender' (low coupling); instead, it receives an interface for sending notifications.","visualDirection": "Draw two boxes: one labeled 'High Cohesion' with tightly related methods inside, another labeled 'Low Coupling' with loose connections to external modules","sourceIds": ["rit-csci-142"]
    },
    {
      "title": "7. Exception Handling and Control Flow",
      "claim": "Exceptions interrupt normal control flow until a matching handler processes them or they propagate, allowing robust error management in object-oriented programs","example": "When dividing by zero in a financial calculation, raise a ZeroDivisionError; catch it in a try-except block to log the error and return a default value.","visualDirection": "Show a code snippet with a try-except block, highlighting the exception being raised and the handler executing, with a visual indicator of flow diversion","sourceIds": ["python-language-reference", "rit-csci-142"]
    },
    {
      "title": "8. Input/Output Streams and GUI Basics",
      "claim": "Input and output streams provide a unified interface for reading from and writing to various sources, while graphical user interfaces enable interactive program interaction","example": "Read user input via sys.stdin stream, process it, and display results using a simple Tkinter window with a label and button.","visualDirection": "Display a mock IDE window with a console pane showing input/output streams and a separate GUI pane with a button and text field","sourceIds": ["rit-csci-142"]
    },
    {
      "title": "9. Modern IDE Integration",
      "claim": "A modern integrated software development environment (IDE) combines code editing, debugging, and project management, essential for efficient object-oriented development","example": "Using PyCharm, a student can set breakpoints, inspect object attributes in the debugger, and refactor code with a single click.","visualDirection": "Screenshot-style graphic of an IDE interface showing the editor, debugger panel, and project explorer, with annotations pointing to key features","sourceIds": ["rit-csci-142"]
    },
    {
      "title": "10. Practical Analysis Checklist",
      "claim": "Correctness arguments, tests, tracing, and debugging compare intended behavior with observable results, including boundary and error cases, ensuring reliable software","example": "Before submitting a project, run unit tests for edge cases (e.g., empty lists, null objects) and trace execution to verify exception handling paths.","visualDirection": "Present a checklist graphic with items: 'Define Pre/Post Conditions', 'Write Unit Tests', 'Trace Execution', 'Review Boundary Cases', each with a checkbox","sourceIds": ["opendsa-programming-foundations", "python-language-reference"]
    }
  ]
}
